  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "The number of points with positive rational coordinates selected from the set of points in the \\(xy\\)-plane such that \\(x+y\\leq5,\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(9\\)",
    "B": "\\(10\\)",
    "C": "\\(14\\)",
    "D": "\\(15\\)",
    "E": "infinite"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Rational coordinates allow fractions, so we can keep one coordinate fixed and produce endlessly many choices for the other. For every positive integer \\(n\\), consider\n\\[\nP_n=\\left(1,\\frac1n\\right).\n\\]\nBoth coordinates are positive rational numbers, and\n\\[\n1+\\frac1n\\leq2<5,\n\\]\nso every \\(P_n\\) satisfies the required inequality. These points are distinct: if \\(P_n=P_m\\), their second coordinates give \\(1/n=1/m\\), which forces \\(n=m\\).\n\nThere are infinitely many positive integers \\(n\\), hence infinitely many distinct admissible points. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{infinite}}\\), choice E.",
